WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.480
Speaker 1: Welcome to the show, the big news
over the last 24 hours, three guilty verdicts

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.410
in the killing of George Floyd by former police
officer Derek Shervin.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.059
We covered it all live yesterday.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:18.250
The verdict coming right around 5:00 p.m.
Eastern time, 4:00 p.m. local time, Central

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:25.529
Time in Minnesota, 10 hours of jury deliberations
before three out of three guilty verdicts.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:32.029
I was not expecting this second degree murder,
guilty, third degree murder, guilty and second

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:34.910
degree manslaughter, guilty.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.450
Let's go to the reading of the verdict by
the court.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.890
Speaker 2: Please be seated.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:59.690
Speaker 3: Members of the jury, I understand
you have a verdict.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:09.120
Members of the jury I to read the verdicts
as they will appear in the permanent records

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:15.040
of the 4th Judicial District, state of Minnesota,
county of Hennepin District Court, Fourth

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.800
Judicial District, state of Minnesota, plaintiff
versus Derrick Michael Shervin, defendant,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:27.950
verdict count one court file number twenty
seven S.R. two zero one two six four six.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:32.380
We the jury in the above entitled manner as
to count one unintentional second degree murder

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.500
while committing a felony, find the defendant
guilty.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.500
This verdict agreed to this 20th day of April.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.200
Twenty twenty one that one forty four p.m.
signed juror foreperson.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.760
Juror number nineteen.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:50.140
Same caption, verdict, count two, we, the
jury in the above entitled manner as to count

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.450
to third degree murder, perpetrating an eminently
dangerous act, find the defendant guilty.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:02.990
This verdict agreed to this 20th day of April
20 21 at one 45 p.m., signed by jury foreperson,

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:09.219
juror number 19, SAM, ception, verdict count
three, we the jury in the above entitled manner

00:02:09.219 --> 00:02:13.939
as to count three, second degree manslaughter,
culpable negligence, creating an unreasonable

00:02:13.939 --> 00:02:15.970
risk, find the defendant guilty.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:20.700
The verdict agreed to this 20th day of April
20 21 at one forty five p.m..

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:28.889
Speaker 1: OK, and then immediately the prosecution
asking for Derek Chauvin to be handcuffed

00:02:28.889 --> 00:02:33.560
and taken into custody pending sentencing,
which we will talk about, which will be about

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.549
eight weeks from now.

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:38.790
Here is Derek Chauvin, let out of court in
handcuffs

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.409
Speaker 3: as their motion behalf of the state.

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:45.790
Speaker 4: The city would move to have the
court to revoke the defendant's bail on, remand

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.959
him into custody pending sentencing.

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:53.260
Speaker 3: Bail is revoked, bond is discharged,
and the defendant is remanded to the custody

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.530
of the Hennepin County sheriff.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:56.530
Anything further?

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:01.549
I thank you.

00:03:01.549 --> 00:03:16.700
Speaker 1: We're adjourned.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:22.510
So many different things to talk about here
immediately, of course, is the question of

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:28.659
sentencing and we have eight weeks until sentencing
and so we'll have more time to talk about

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:30.409
that.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:37.629
But the most serious of the charges, second
degree murder potentially coming with up to

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:43.559
12 and a half years, according to the Minnesota
sentencing guidelines.

00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:50.650
The prosecution has asked for a longer sentence,
saying there were kids present at the scene.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:56.579
What Derrick Shervin did represent that particular
cruelty and that it included an abuse of Chauvin's

00:03:56.579 --> 00:03:59.140
position of authority.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.360
Chauvin is 45 years old and the sentencing
will take place in eight weeks.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:10.209
And we will see if there is the possibility
in total of what would be theoretically close

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:14.319
to a life sentence for Derrick Chauvin or
not.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:21.190
And although one can make the case that his
lawyer, Erik Nelson, failed here to secure

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:28.180
even one not guilty verdict on any of the
of the charges with the chocolate chip cookie

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:33.740
closing statement and all of this other stuff,
the future of Derrick Chauvin's life may well

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.419
actually be in the sentencing and there will
be time for that.

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:41.210
Let's talk about the implications of these
verdicts, though.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:49.750
Is this the start of a change to the perception
of what police officers can get away with?

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:50.979
This is not a happy moment.

00:04:50.979 --> 00:04:55.970
You know, it's not fun for people to go to
prison for killing others.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:05:02.590
It would be better if George Floyd were alive
and there were many right wingers talking

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.870
about, oh, the left is happy now.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.260
The left is thrilled about what's going on.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.550
It's a celebration.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:13.490
It's not a celebration that someone now goes
to prison.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.150
It's the recognition, the recognition.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:23.900
I guess I would say that given the circumstances,
there will be something approximating accountability.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:29.639
It's not even really justice because George
Floyd is dead and that's irreversible.

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:36.229
But we're going to talk about, you know, in
a moment, there's also the realization that

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:43.990
this is a weird situation where the state
of Minnesota was prosecuting someone that

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:45.780
the state trained, in essence.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.979
Now, I know, you know, local police departments
do their own training, but it's under the

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:52.979
guidance and under the framework of the state
of Minnesota.

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:59.039
So the state by prosecuting Shervin, is in
a sense dealing with a problem it created

00:05:59.039 --> 00:06:02.129
as part of a policing culture that they have
a role in.

00:06:02.129 --> 00:06:07.219
And now I understand literally in a literal
sense, the prosecutors aren't involved in

00:06:07.219 --> 00:06:13.280
police training, but it's the idea of the
state and the state here involved on both

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:14.280
ends.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:21.039
And there's also the statistical reality that
officers who kill people tend to avoid prosecution

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:23.780
or if they're prosecuted, they tend to be
acquitted.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:31.789
Since 2005, out of 140 cases of police being
arrested and charged with murder or manslaughter

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:38.409
as a result of an on duty shooting, out of
the 140 cases, 97 have concluded, the other

00:06:38.409 --> 00:06:39.580
43 are still pending.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:40.580
So we can't say.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:46.000
But of the 97 that concluded, there were seven
murder convictions.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:55.379
So when you understand that there is a question
here of is this possibly the start to a change

00:06:55.379 --> 00:07:01.310
in the culture around what happens in these
cases?

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.800
Often they get reduced to lesser offenses.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.629
You have dismissals and then, of course, you
also have acquittals and plea deals and different

00:07:07.629 --> 00:07:08.629
things.

00:07:08.629 --> 00:07:10.349
So that is the result.

00:07:10.349 --> 00:07:12.620
I did not expect three guilty verdicts.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:18.990
I think I was probably leaning on kind of
a mixed verdict of not guilty and some guilty

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:22.270
guilty on all three, eight weeks until sentencing.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:29.270
And the next question is, what, if anything,
does this say about racism?

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:30.270
Bigger picture.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:08:33.610
Let's talk about 

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:38.029
that next.

